---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import PythonWorkflow from '../../../components/tutorials/PythonWorkflow';
import Math from '../../../components/common/Math.astro';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Symbolic Analysis with SymPy"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Code Generation Workflows', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'SymPy & Bode Plots' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Simulation with CasADi', href: '/tutorials/workflows/casadi' }}
  next={{ label: 'Custom Templates', href: '/tutorials/workflows/custom-templates' }}
>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The SymPy backend generates symbolic expressions that you can manipulate analytically in Python.
    Unlike numerical backends, SymPy output preserves the full symbolic structure of your model,
    making it ideal for transfer function derivation, stability analysis, Laplace-domain work, and
    generating publication-quality equations.
  </p>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    Below is a simple DC motor plant model. Click <strong>Compile</strong> to generate symbolic ODE
    expressions with the SymPy backend, then use the resulting Python script to extract the transfer
    function and produce a Bode plot. You can also open the full workflow in Google Colab.
  </p>

  <PythonWorkflow
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model DCMotor
  Real omega(start = 0) "Angular velocity (rad/s)";
  Real i(start = 0) "Armature current (A)";
  input Real V "Applied voltage (V)";
  parameter Real R = 1.0 "Resistance (Ohm)";
  parameter Real L = 0.5 "Inductance (H)";
  parameter Real Ke = 0.01 "Back-EMF constant";
  parameter Real Kt = 0.01 "Torque constant";
  parameter Real J = 0.01 "Inertia (kg*m^2)";
  parameter Real b = 0.1 "Friction (N*m*s)";
equation
  L * der(i) = V - R * i - Ke * omega;
  J * der(omega) = Kt * i - b * omega;
end DCMotor;`}
    modelName="DCMotor"
    backend="sympy"
    pipInstalls={['sympy', 'matplotlib', 'numpy']}
    title="SymPy Bode Plot Workflow"
    pythonTemplate={`{generated_code}

import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Define Laplace variable and parameter symbols
s = sp.Symbol('s')
R, L, Ke, Kt, J, b = sp.symbols('R L Ke Kt J b', positive=True)

# State-space matrices from the symbolic ODE
# State vector: [i, omega], Input: V
# der(i)     = (-R/L)*i + (-Ke/L)*omega + (1/L)*V
# der(omega) = (Kt/J)*i + (-b/J)*omega
A = sp.Matrix([
    [-R/L, -Ke/L],
    [Kt/J, -b/J]
])
B = sp.Matrix([1/L, 0])
C = sp.Matrix([[0, 1]])  # output = omega

# Transfer function: G(s) = C * (sI - A)^-1 * B
I = sp.eye(2)
G = C * (s * I - A).inv() * B
G_simplified = sp.simplify(G[0, 0])
print("G(s) =", G_simplified)

# Substitute numerical parameter values
params = {R: 1.0, L: 0.5, Ke: 0.01, Kt: 0.01, J: 0.01, b: 0.1}
G_num = G_simplified.subs(params)

# Create a callable: G(j*omega)
G_func = sp.lambdify(s, G_num, 'numpy')

# Frequency range
freq = np.logspace(-2, 4, 1000)
jw = 1j * freq
response = G_func(jw)

# Bode magnitude plot
fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(8, 6), sharex=True)
ax1.semilogx(freq, 20 * np.log10(np.abs(response)))
ax1.set_ylabel('Magnitude (dB)')
ax1.set_title('Bode Plot: V -> omega')
ax1.grid(True, which='both', alpha=0.3)

# Bode phase plot
ax2.semilogx(freq, np.degrees(np.angle(response)))
ax2.set_ylabel('Phase (deg)')
ax2.set_xlabel('Frequency (rad/s)')
ax2.grid(True, which='both', alpha=0.3)

plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig('dc_motor_bode.png', dpi=150)
plt.show()`}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">How the Workflow Works</h2>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    When you click <strong>Compile</strong>, the Modelica model is compiled to symbolic SymPy
    expressions and injected at the top of the Python script. The rest of the script then:
  </p>

  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4 space-y-1">
    <li>
      Constructs state-space matrices <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">A</code>,
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">B</code>,
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">C</code> from the symbolic ODE.
    </li>
    <li>
      Derives the transfer function <Math tex={`G(s) = C\\,(sI - A)^{-1}\\,B`} />
      from voltage input to angular velocity output.
    </li>
    <li>
      Substitutes numerical parameter values and uses
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">sp.lambdify</code> to create a fast
      NumPy-compatible callable.
    </li>
    <li>
      Generates a Bode plot (magnitude and phase) over four decades of frequency.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
    This workflow lets you go from a Modelica model to a full frequency-domain analysis without
    manually deriving equations. Any changes to the model are automatically reflected in the
    transfer function and Bode plot when you re-compile.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
